=== EXPERIMENT 1, 2, 3 RESULTS (2026-08-18) ===

EXPERIMENT 1 (QEMU debug flags):
  Applied: -d int,guest_errors,cpu_reset -D [run_dir]/qemu-debug.log
  Result: qemu-debug.log contains ONLY "CPU Reset" register dumps (46 resets
  captured, 1288 log lines = 46 × 28 lines per reset dump). The register
  state shows EIP=0xfff0 (real mode reset vector), CR3=0 (no page table),
  CR0=0x60000010 (PE set, PG clear). This is the INITIAL CPU state
  before OVMF starts, NOT the state when QEMU is killed by the framework.
  No exception interrupts, no unimp accesses, no guest errors captured.
  → NO FAULT VISIBLE IN QEMU DEBUG LOG (QEMU only logs at reset events,
    not during execution).

EXPERIMENT 2 (4G QEMU RAM):
  Applied: -m 4G (was -m 1G)
  Result: 84 occurrences of "Booting all finished" across 3 retries
  (vs 41 with 1G = ~2x as many boots per retry). Same warnings
  ("device memory ... is unavailable (limited untyped cnode range)"
  and "unable to register range as RAM: [0x80c000..0x810000) [0x900000..0xa00000)").
  ZERO occurrences of "Genode v" banner. QEMU exits; test times out.
  → INCONCLUSIVE: more RAM did NOT resolve the issue. The issue is
    NOT untyped list exhaustion from low-RAM (the 2 failing ranges
    [16KB and 1MB] are too small to fit untyped objects of the typical
    seL4 object size; this is a structural fragmentation issue, not a
    capacity issue).

EXPERIMENT 3 (pc/i440fx machine type):
  Applied: -machine pc (was -machine q35)
  Result: 81 occurrences of "Booting all finished". Same warnings.
  ZERO occurrences of "Genode v". QEMU exits; test times out.
  → INCONCLUSIVE: different MMIO layout (i440fx vs q35) did NOT resolve
    the issue. The fragmentation pattern is similar.

=== FAULT-VS-HANG VERDICT ===

HANG (not fault). Confirmed by:
  1. bender DOES load seL4 (Kernel loaded to 0x200000)
  2. seL4 DOES drop to user space ("Booting all finished")
  3. The 2 untyped_pool warnings appear (so initial_untyped_pool runs)
  4. No "Genode v" banner ever appears (core hangs in
     bootstrap_component after Platform construction, before
     log("Genode ", version_string) at main.cc:126)
  5. No page fault / triple fault / GP / unimp captured in QEMU debug log
  6. No QEMU exit message indicating a fault (just SIGKILL from the
     test framework after 120s timeout)

The HANG is between:
  - end of initial_untyped_pool (which prints "unable to register..."
    warnings at line 141/148 of platform.cc)
  - the log("Genode ", version_string) call at main.cc:126

This is the "core init in [Platform construction, log call]" window.
The core does init_exception_handling, init_core_signal_transmitter,
init_page_fault_handling, and allocates various services. The HANG
must be in one of these. The most likely candidate is
init_page_fault_handling which involves the VM subsystem and could
deadlock if some init_untyped callback failed silently.

=== SINGLE RECOMMENDED NEXT ACTION ===

Increase CONFIG_MAX_NUM_BOOTINFO_UNTYPED_CAPS from 160 to a larger
value (e.g. 1024). The 2 "unable to register range as RAM" warnings
show that the cnode is too small to register all UEFI-mode RAM
fragments (16KB at 0x80c000-0x810000 and 1MB at 0x900000-0xa00000).
Genode core's initial_untyped_pool creates one untyped cnode entry
per memory range; with 160 slots and 50+ high-MMIO untypeds, only
~110 slots are left for RAM. These 2 small fragments might be the
last to fit and could fail in a way that blocks core's init silently.

This requires a base-sel4 kernel rebuild (kconf), so it's out of W1
scope. The next phase should rebuild base-sel4 with a higher
CONFIG_MAX_NUM_BOOTINFO_UNTYPED_CAPS (e.g. 1024 or 2048) and
re-run W1.
